diff --git a/Assets/MATE ENGINE - Referee/README.md b/Assets/MATE ENGINE - Referee/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..039bb79
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Assets/MATE ENGINE - Referee/README.md	
@@ -0,0 +1,241 @@
+# MATE ENGINE - Referee
+
+Additions to the MateEngineX Linux port that let another program drive the avatar over HTTP:
+
+- show a line in the avatar's speech bubble while its mouth moves (`say` / `talk` / `stop`);
+- play and stop the built-in custom dances (`dance` / `dance_stop`);
+- export dance mods and build the player from the command line.
+
+It was written for a live debate fact-checker whose "referee" interjects by voice. The bridge itself knows
+nothing about debates. **The avatar never plays audio and never sends anything back.** Another program (in our
+case a browser tab) plays the voice and reports whether it is audible. The avatar only mirrors that state.
+
+Everything is **off by default**. Without the flags below the program behaves exactly like upstream.
+
+## Enabling the speech bridge
+
+Any one of these turns it on:
+
+| How | Example |
+|---|---|
+| Command line (also sets the server) | `./launch.sh -speech-bridge http://127.0.0.1:8000` |
+| Environment variable | `MEX_SPEECH_BRIDGE=http://127.0.0.1:8000 ./launch.sh` (`1` keeps the configured server; `0` turns it off even if the file enables it) |
+| Config file | `{"enabled": true}` in `speech_bridge.json` (below) |
+
+Precedence: command line, then environment, then config file, then defaults. On start the log (`Player.log`, or
+stdout through `launch.sh`) shows `[SpeechBridge] enabled: <settings>`. It then logs only connection state
+changes and one line per command.
+
+### Config file
+
+The file is `Application.persistentDataPath/speech_bridge.json`, which on Linux is
+`~/.config/unity3d/Shinymoon/MateEngineX/speech_bridge.json`. It is optional, and any field you leave out keeps
+its default:
+
+```json
+{
+  "enabled": false,
+  "server": "http://127.0.0.1:8000",
+  "pollPath": "/face/mate/next",
+  "fontSize": 40,
+  "bubbleWidth": 720,
+  "lingerSeconds": 3,
+  "stopDanceOnSay": true,
+  "verbose": false
+}
+```
+
+| Field | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `enabled` | Start the bridge without the command-line flag |
+| `server` | Base URL of the server. Overridden by `-speech-bridge <url>` or `MEX_SPEECH_BRIDGE` |
+| `pollPath` | Path of the long-poll route |
+| `fontSize`, `bubbleWidth` | Bubble size in pixels. ME's own bubbles are 26 and 600; the defaults here are sized for a projector |
+| `lingerSeconds` | How long the bubble stays after the voice stops |
+| `stopDanceOnSay` | Stop a running dance when a new line arrives |
+| `verbose` | Log every poll and every `talk` |
+
+## Wire protocol v1
+
+This is the agreed text, copied verbatim. "Bus", `Interjection`, `TTSState`, `Control` and `SessionStart` are
+the server's own event names; the bridge only sees the commands.
+
+```
+SHARED WIRE PROTOCOL (server <-> Mate Engine; both tracks MUST implement exactly this; JsonUtility on the C# side cannot handle nulls/dicts, so every field is always present):
+GET /face/mate/next?after=<cursor:int>&epoch=<string>&wait=<seconds:int, default 10, max 25>
+ -> 200 {"v":1,"epoch":"<8 hex>","cursor":<int>,"cmds":[Cmd,...]}
+ Cmd = {"op":"say"|"talk"|"stop"|"dance"|"dance_stop","id":"","text":"","audio":"","title":"","on":false,"clear":false}
+ - say: id=Interjection.id, text=Interjection.text (exact, shown in the bubble), audio = relative URL of the WAV for that line ("/face/tts?text=<urlencoded>") or "" when no TTS provider is configured.
+ - talk: id=TTSState.interjection_id or "", on=TTSState.speaking (the browser ?audio=1 tab is the ONLY audio player and the only TTSState producer; ME just follows it to move the mouth).
+ - stop: clear=true for Control mute=true, Control director=false, SessionStart (remove bubble + close mouth); clear=false for Control tts=false (close mouth, keep bubble).
+ - dance: title = dance title to play ("" = first available); dance_stop: stop dancing. These come only from POST /face/mate/dance {"title":""} and POST /face/mate/dance/stop (operator routes, face-only, not bus contracts).
+ Rules: if after<0, or epoch != server epoch, or after > bus.last_seq (server restarted) -> respond immediately {cursor: bus.last_seq, cmds: []} (never replay). Interjections older than 4 s at delivery are dropped. Long-poll: subscribe and scan bus.history in the same synchronous tick so nothing is missed; return as soon as >=1 cmd exists or wait expires. Each poll marks ME alive.
+ ME publishes nothing to the server (pure consumer).
+```
+
+### What the avatar does with each command
+
+**Polling**
+- The first poll uses cursor `-1`. After a resync it takes the cursor and epoch from the response.
+- When the epoch changes (the server restarted), the bridge resyncs and replays nothing.
+- On errors it backs off from 0.5 s to 5 s. Each request times out after 30 s.
+- After an empty answer that came back in under 0.5 s (a resync, or a server that does not wait) it pauses 0.5 s.
+
+**`say`**
+- Ignores an id it has already shown (it remembers the last 50 ids).
+- Replaces the current bubble and, if `stopDanceOnSay` is set, stops a running dance.
+- Builds the bubble exactly like ME's random-message bubble (same container, sprite, font, colours, padding and
+  material) at the configured size, with no typing sound. The text is shown verbatim; rich text is off.
+- If something else destroys the bubble, the line is dropped.
+- The text appears at about 18 characters per second while the voice plays.
+- If no `talk on` arrives within 3 s, it shows the whole line at once and removes it after
+  max(4, words / 2.5 + 3) s.
+
+**`talk`**
+- `on` sets the animator's `isTalking` bool, which drives ME's own talking animation. It is re-applied every
+  frame, because ME's scripts may reset it.
+- `off` closes the mouth, completes the text and removes the bubble after `lingerSeconds`.
+- The mouth is forced shut after 12 s without `off`.
+
+**`stop`**
+- Closes the mouth.
+- With `clear` it also removes the bubble.
+- Without `clear` the bubble stays for the bubble-only time.
+
+**`dance`**
+- Plays the installed dance whose title (its file name without extension) matches. With an empty or unknown
+  title it plays the first dance.
+- On first use it turns off the dance player's multi-instance sync for the session, which removes the 1.5 s
+  lead-in of menu plays and auto-next (the bridge's own plays are not delayed).
+
+**`dance_stop`** stops the dance.
+
+**Scene objects**
+- The avatar, animator, bubble style and dance player are looked up again for every command, because loading a
+  VRM replaces them.
+- A failure is logged once and never stops the polling.
+
+## Dances
+
+**Player fixes** in `MATE ENGINE - Scripts/AvatarHandlers/AvatarDancePlayer.cs` (marked `[Referee fork]`):
+- A dance whose bundle fails to load now logs the reason and leaves the "Wait For Dance" pose instead of
+  freezing in it.
+- Every play logs the chosen clip and audio.
+- Every rescan logs how many dances were found, and their names.
+- It never picks a controller-less helper Animator (`MateEngineAPI/CustomDances/ADS_ProxyRoot`) as the avatar, and it
+  keeps looking until the avatar is visible. Before, a dance started while the avatar was hidden (first-run tutorial)
+  failed silently and every later one did too. When no avatar is found it now logs a warning.
+
+**Where dances come from:** `.me` dance mods in `~/.config/unity3d/Shinymoon/MateEngineX/Mods/` or
+`<build>/MateEngineX_Data/StreamingAssets/CustomDances/`.
+Community `.unity3d` dances built for Windows with Unity 2021 or 2022 play as they are on this Linux build (tested
+with "Zombie Dance" and "Motion-DoodleDance"), so they need no re-export.
+
+**Exporting a dance mod**
+- **Editor:** MateEngine > ME Mod Exporter > Dance Mod. When the selected target (upstream default
+  `StandaloneWindows64`) has no build support installed, it switches to the editor's active standalone target,
+  which is `StandaloneLinux64` on this port. Exporting for a target without build support reports an error
+  instead of writing a broken `.me`.
+- **Headless:** the same exporter code. Close any editor that has the project open, then run:
+
+  ```bash
+  ~/Unity/Hub/Editor/6000.2.6f2/Editor/Unity -batchmode -quit -projectPath "$PWD" -logFile export.log \
+    -executeMethod DanceModCli.Export --dance-clip "Assets/<path>/<clip>.anim" \
+    [--dance-audio "Assets/<path>/<song>.wav"] --dance-name MyDance [--dance-out ExportedMods] \
+    [--dance-author <name>] [--dance-mmd-author <name>] [--dance-target StandaloneLinux64]
+  ```
+
+  The output is `<dance-out>/<dance-name>.me`: a zip of `dance.bundle`, the bundle manifests and
+  `dance_meta.json`. Without `--dance-audio` the dance is silent and lasts as long as the clip. The command exits
+  with code 1 on failure.
+
+**Autoplay test hook**
+- Usage: `--dance-autoplay <title>` (or `MEX_DANCE_AUTOPLAY=<title>`). `'*'` (quoted, so the shell does not expand it) means the first dance.
+- Shortly after start it plays the dance and logs `[DanceAutoplay] PlayIndex -> True`.
+- It then logs `[DanceAutoplay] IsPlaying=True clip=<name>/<len>s audio=<name> playing=<bool> t=<time>` once a
+  second for 8 s.
+- Finally it stops the dance and logs `[DanceAutoplay] after StopPlay IsPlaying=False`.
+- In a fresh config (for example a separate `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` for a test run) the first-run tutorial hides the avatar,
+  so nothing dances. Set `"tutorialDone": true` in `<config>/unity3d/Shinymoon/MateEngineX/settings.json` first, or
+  finish the tutorial once.
+
+Dance music plays through the speakers. If a microphone is listening, mute or avoid dances while it is.
+
+## Building
+
+```bash
+./build-referee.sh <output-dir> [--scripts-only] [--dev]
+```
+
+- It runs Unity in batch mode with `-executeMethod RefereeBuild.Build`. That method builds the Addressables
+  content (the localisation tables), then the Linux player with LZ4. `--scripts-only` recompiles the scripts into
+  an existing build, and `--dev` makes a development build.
+- The result is `<output-dir>/Payload/MateEngineX.x86_64` plus `launch.sh`, and `<output-dir>/install.sh`.
+- The log goes to `<output-dir>/build.log`. `RefereeBuild` writes every build error to that log and exits with
+  code 1 on failure; the script exits with Unity's exit code and prints the last 20 `RefereeBuild:` and compiler
+  error lines.
+- Environment variables:
+
+  | Variable | Use |
+  |---|---|
+  | `UNITY` | Path to the editor binary |
+  | `LIBXML2_SHIM_DIR` | A folder with `libxml2.so.2`, used only if the system has none |
+  | `PREBUILT_PAYLOAD` | A released `Payload/` folder to copy the gitignored `libStandaloneFileBrowser.so` from |
+  | `NICE` | Niceness of the editor process |
+
+- Unlike `build.sh` it does not pass `-nographics`. The upstream README still recommends GUI builds for releases,
+  because CLI builds "may see some abnormal behaviours"; test a CLI build before relying on it.
+
+## Trying it without any other software
+
+```bash
+python3 tools/mock_mate_server.py 8765          # Python 3 standard library only
+./launch.sh -speech-bridge http://127.0.0.1:8765
+```
+
+Every 25 s the mock says a line, opens and closes the mouth, plays the first installed dance and stops it.
+
+For the cleanest demo, turn off ME's random messages, Minecraft messages and Discord presence. Set its pet, effect
+and menu volumes to 0, and keep `allowedApps` empty, so that playing audio does not trigger auto-dancing.
+
+## Files
+
+| File | Purpose |
+|---|---|
+| `Runtime/SpeechBridge.cs` | Start-up hook, long-poll, command handling, bubble timing, mouth |
+| `Runtime/SpeechBridgeConfig.cs` | `speech_bridge.json`, environment variable and command-line settings |
+| `Runtime/SpeechWire.cs` | Protocol v1 data types |
+| `Runtime/SpeechBubble.cs` | The projector-sized bubble, built like ME's own (it mirrors LLMUnity's `Bubble`) |
+| `Runtime/RefereeArgs.cs` | Command-line and environment helpers |
+| `Runtime/DanceAutoplay.cs` | `--dance-autoplay` test hook |
+| `Editor/DanceModCli.cs` | Headless dance-mod export |
+| `Editor/RefereeBuild.cs` | Command-line player build |
+| `../Editor/ModExporterWindow.cs` | (modified) default target, missing-target error, shared dance export |
+| `../MATE ENGINE - Scripts/AvatarHandlers/AvatarDancePlayer.cs` | (modified) the dance player fixes above |
+| `/build-referee.sh`, `/tools/mock_mate_server.py` | Build script and mock server at the repository root |
+
+This folder has no assembly definition on purpose: the code uses ME's classes, so it must compile into
+`Assembly-CSharp` (an assembly definition cannot reference it). The runtime scripts use only public types, so
+`Runtime/` also compiles as a separate DLL against a released build's `MateEngineX_Data/Managed/` (the
+no-rebuild fallback). No scene, prefab or project setting is changed: everything starts from
+`[RuntimeInitializeOnLoadMethod]`.
+
